= How movement works

Every technique in this book solves one of a few mechanical problems. How do you keep your weight over your feet? How do you hang on your skeleton instead of your muscles? How do you stop your body rotating off the wall? How do you put your body where a hold wants it? Once you understand these ideas, the named techniques stop looking like a long list. You can predict which one a problem needs.

== Centre of gravity and base of support

Your #ix("Centre of gravity", main: true)#jt("centre of gravity") is the point your weight acts through. When you stand upright, it sits in your pelvis, near your navel #src("B16"). It moves when you bend, reach or raise a leg. Gravity pulls straight down through it. Picture a #jt("plumb line") hanging straight down from it. It shows at a glance where your weight goes.

Your #ix("Base of support", main: true)#jt("base of support") is the set of points holding you: footholds, handholds, a knee or a heel. On the ground it is the area between your feet. On a wall, use it like this:

- If the plumb line falls *over or between your footholds*, your feet carry your weight. Your hands only stop you falling backwards. That costs little grip #src("B8", "B17").
- If the plumb line falls *outside your feet*, your fingers must hold you up and in #src("B7", "B8"). Every second in that position costs strength.

The steeper the wall, the more weight moves from your feet to your hands. Measurements show this even between a slight slab and a slight overhang #src("B8"). So the first question for any position is: _where is my weight, and what carries it?_ Most footwork and body-positioning techniques move your hips so the answer is "my feet".

#figure(image("../figures/concept-balance.svg"), caption: [(a) Hips centred between the feet: both feet share the weight. (b) Moving the hips over the left foot puts all the weight on it. The right foot can then move without the hands taking over.], kind: image, supplement: [Figure]) <fig-balance>

Good climbers share a rhythm: *shift the hips first, then move the limb*#ix("Hips!shifting first") #src("B22"). Before you lift a foot, move your hips over the other foot. Before you reach, move your hips under or beside the hold you reach from. Then the reach doesn't drag your weight off your feet. Expert climbers shift their weight from side to side more than novices do#ix("Experts and novices!weight shifting") #src("B9"). #tref("weight-shift"), #tref("precise-footwork") and #tref("foot-swap") all depend on this. So does #tref("slab", body: [slab climbing]).

#filmstrip("filmstrip-hips-first.svg", [Hips first, then the foot], [Watch the plumb line: the free foot moves only while the line falls on the other foot.])

== Straight arms: hang on your skeleton

Muscle holds a bent arm bent. Lock your elbow at ninety degrees. Your biceps and forearms then work all the time to hold it there. Straighten the arm. The pull now runs along the bones. Your elbow muscles can then rest #src("B12", "B30"). Your shoulder#ix("Shoulders!on straight arms") muscles still hold the joint in place #src("B50"). That is why coaches teach #tref("straight-arms") from the start #src("B1").

On long problems your forearms #jt("pump")#ix("Pump!reducing"): they swell, ache and weaken. Straightening your arms whenever you are not pulling reduces that load #src("B22"). It also forces you to move with your legs, because a straight arm can't pull.

#figure(image("../figures/concept-straight-arms.svg"), caption: [(a) Bent arms on an overhang: the arm muscles hold the body up. (b) Straight arms, bent legs. The body hangs on the arm bones. The legs do the pushing.], kind: image, supplement: [Figure])

Straight arms are not a rule for every moment. You bend your arms to pull through a move or to #tref("lock-off", body: [lock off]). The skill is to straighten them again as soon as the move is done. Then set up the next move with straight arms: move the feet, turn the hips.

== Hips and the wall

How far your hips#ix("Hips!distance from the wall") are from the wall decides how much weight goes through your feet. The right distance depends on the wall angle.

- *On an overhang*#ix("Overhangs!hip position"), your feet are below and in front of you. Gravity pulls your hips away from the wall. The further they sag out, the more your feet slide off and the more your arms carry #src("B7", "B16"). Bring your hips in, square or by turning one hip to the wall. That moves your weight towards your toes #src("B28"). Pulling with your toes helps too. Both press your feet onto the holds. See #tref("hip-turn"), #tref("toe-pull") and #tref("overhang").
- *On a vertical wall*#ix("Vertical walls!body position"), stand tall, with your body straight and over your feet #src("B17"). Don't sink low to straighten your arms: it moves weight off your feet onto your fingers #src("B8").
- *On a slab*#ix("Slab technique!hip position"), the wall leans away from you. Hugging it pushes your feet off the holds. Stand straight over your feet with your hips slightly *away* from the wall. Your weight then presses down through your shoes #src("B17"). See #tref("slab").

"Close" does not mean "as tight as you can". Pulling in too tight on an overhang blocks your turn and unloads your feet #src("B48"). On a test wall, expert climbers held their centre of gravity slightly farther from the wall than novices did#ix("Experts and novices!distance from the wall") #src("B9").

#figure(image("../figures/concept-hips-in.svg"), caption: [On a 30° overhang. (a) Hips out: the plumb line falls far from the feet, so the arms carry the weight. (b) Hips in: the weight moves towards the feet. They stay on.], kind: image, supplement: [Figure])

== Holds have a direction

A hold is not just "good" or "bad". It has a surface you can pull on. It only works when you pull away from the way that surface faces. A jug works from below. An #tref("undercling") works from above, pulling upward. A #tref("sidepull") whose gripping edge faces left works when your body is to its right, leaning away from it. Used with your body on its left, the same hold becomes a #tref("gaston"). A #tref("sloper") works only when your body hangs below it, arm straight #src("B31"). So the holds dictate where your body goes. #ix("Direction of pull", main: true)

This is the most useful idea for #ix("Route reading")reading problems. Look at which way every hold faces. Then ask where your body must be for each hold to work. Chapter 2 turns this into a method. Chapter 5 describes each hold type.

Three kinds of force come out of this:

- *Pulling*, towards you or downward, as on a #tref("jug").
- *Pushing*, as when you press a palm on a #tref("volume", body: [volume]) or #tref("mantle", body: [mantle]) onto a ledge.
- *Opposition*: two forces pushing or pulling against each other. Two hands squeeze a block (#tref("compression")). A hand pulls one way while a foot pushes the other (#tref("layback")). Your feet push apart in a corner (#tref("stemming")). Opposition makes holds work that are useless on their own #src("B1", "B14"). See #tref("opposition").

== Rotation and the barn door

Hang from two points, say your left hand and your left foot. Your body can rotate#ix("Rotation") around the line between them, like a door on its hinges. If your centre of gravity sits to the side of that #jt("hinge")#ix("Hinge"), gravity swings your body open #src("B8"). This is the #tref("barn-door"). It explains many falls that seem to have no cause.

#figure(image("../figures/concept-barn-door.svg"), caption: [(a) The left hand and left foot form a hinge. Reaching right swings the body open. (b) A back flag. The free right leg crosses behind the left and presses the wall on the far side of the hinge. The centre of gravity now sits on the hinge.], kind: image, supplement: [Figure])

Every fix for a barn door does one of these things #src("B11", "B13", "B62"):

+ *Put weight or a contact on the far side of the hinge.* Press your free foot on the wall there, even without a hold. If the free leg is already on that side, that is a #tref("flag"). If not, cross it behind (#tref("back-flag")) or in front (#tref("inside-flag")) #src("B26").
+ *Move your centre of gravity onto the hinge.* Shift your hips over the standing foot.
+ *Change the hinge.* Swap feet so the hand and foot you use are on opposite sides: left hand with right foot. That diagonal resists rotation #src("B11"). A #tref("backstep") or #tref("drop-knee") does this with your hips turned in.
+ *Beat the swing.* Move fast enough that the rotation has no time to build #src("B62").

A good default for any move is the diagonal: *opposite hand and foot*#ix("Opposite hand and foot", main: true) #src("B11"). When a problem forces same-side hand and foot, expect to need one of these fixes.

== Static and dynamic

A #jt("static")#ix("Static movement", main: true) move is one you could stop at any point: your body stays in balance throughout. A #jt("dynamic")#ix("Dynamic movement", main: true) move uses #jt("momentum")#ix("Momentum"). You move fast enough that your hand reaches the next hold before you have time to fall. Static movement is precise and controllable. Dynamic movement saves strength when the static version needs a hard lock-off. It is the only option when the next hold is out of static reach. From about V4 indoors you need both, and much of the skill lies in choosing. Chapter 6 covers the dynamic side, starting with the #tref("deadpoint").

== Tension: connecting hands and feet

On steep ground your trunk connects your hands to your feet. If your trunk is slack, your feet swing off when you move a hand, however good the footholds are. #tref("body-tension") is the skill of pulling your feet into the wall with the muscles of your trunk, hips and legs #src("B71"). Your body then acts as one rigid piece between the holds. The physiotherapist Jason Hooper#ix("Hooper, Jason") argues that this is mostly technique and coordination, not raw #jt("core") strength#ix("Core!strength vs. technique") #src("B51"). Tension is why good climbers can stand on tiny footholds on an overhang. It is also why #tref("heel-hook", body: [heel hooks]) and #tref("toe-hook", body: [toe hooks]) work: they pull your body in rather than push it.

== Three questions for every move <three-questions>

#keypoint[
  + *Where is my weight?* Can I get my hips over a foot, or closer to the wall?
  + *Which way do these holds pull?* Where must my body be for each hold to work?
  + *What stops me rotating?* If I let go with one hand, what holds me in, and where can a free foot go?
]

Ask these three questions on the ground, before you pull on. Ask them again whenever you fall off. The answers point straight to a technique. The troubleshooting appendix lists the techniques by symptom.
